This argument uses Nolan's released-film dialogue through unofficial subtitle transcriptions rather than an authorized published screenplay; wording should be checked against an official script if one becomes available. The paper also treats ``chance'' and ``consequence'' as deliberately unstable English terms while relying on a selective rather than exhaustive lexical study of Homeric Greek. A full study would require corpus-level annotation of \textit{moira}, \textit{moros}, \textit{aissa}, \textit{atē}, \textit{atasthalia}, \textit{aitios}, \textit{tisis}, the verbal family of \textit{tunchanein}, and verbs of knowing, inferring, persuading, and recognizing. Finally, the operational language used here, such as ``causal graph,'' ``access control,'' and ``actionable knowledge,'' is analytical reconstruction, not Homeric source syntax. Its value depends on whether it exposes distinctions in the poem without pretending that archaic epic already contained a systems theory.

